WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.990
- Uh-oh! Sam Bankman-Fried
is now Sam Bankman-Felon.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:05.880
Yeah, this was apparently
news to Bankman-Fried.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.590
He was scheduled to testify
in front of Congress

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.960
on December 13th, and
just 24 hours earlier

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.240
while playing video games
in his Bahamas penthouse,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.340
he told interviewers that he would have

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.600
to attend the hearing by Zoom

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.550
because he was quite overbooked.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.860
He also remarked, "I don't
think I will be arrested."

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.300
And maybe when he said overbooked,

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.730
he meant "about to be booked,"
because later that same day,

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.250
authorities arrested Bankman-Fried,
took him into custody,

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.470
and prepared to extradite
him to the United States,

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.750
where he's been indicted for
wire fraud, securities fraud,

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.360
commodities fraud, money laundering,

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.330
and illegal campaign contributions.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:37.380
Classic Pisces.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.330
And now we know that most
of the C-Suite at FTX,

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.980
including Bankman-Fried's confidantes,

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.930
have reached plea agreements
with the government

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:44.250
and are cooperating with the government

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:45.960
against Sam Bankman-Fried.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.960
And this helps explain how
things moved so quickly.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.820
And as we discussed in our last video,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.890
it looked like there was going
to be a jurisdictional tussle

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.810
between regulators who would win the right

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.310
to prosecute Bankman-Fried.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.110
Now we know the regulators decided

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.330
that there was enough crimeing
and torting to go around

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.670
for everyone, with civil
complaints being filed

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.920
by the Securities Exchange
Commission, the SEC,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.530
and the Commodities Futures
Trading Commission, the CFTC,

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.290
as well as criminal charges
by the Department of Justice.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.370
Fortunately for Bankman-Fried,

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.110
he's used to living with tons of roommates

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:14.739
and wearing the same clothes every day.

00:01:14.739 --> 00:01:18.030
(funky music)

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.890
Now, before we get into
all of the alleged crimes,

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:21.720
and there are a lot of alleged crimes,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:22.980
let's do a quick factual rundown

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.330
because now we have the benefit

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.600
of three detailed proceedings,
two civil complaints,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.640
and one criminal DOJ indictment.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:32.520
Now, the SEC and the CFTC
filed separate civil complaints

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.380
providing additional factual context

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.090
to what is alleged to have gone on here.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:36.923
Now, as you probably know,

00:01:36.923 --> 00:01:38.640
Bankman-Fried was the
founder of Alameda Research,

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.350
which he represented as a separate entity

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.380
from the FTX entities,
especially after Caroline Ellison

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.920
and Sam Trabucco became
co-CEOs of Alameda in 2021.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.750
However, the SEC and CFTC say

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.840
that Bankman-Fried did not hand
off Alameda Research at all

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.420
and that he remained the ultimate
decision-maker at Alameda.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.640
The SEC says that Bankman-Fried
directed investment

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.110
and operational decisions,

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.570
frequently communicated
with Alameda employees,

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:03.270
and had full access to
Alameda's records and databases,

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.580
and the CFTC says that
Bankman-Fried operated all

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.500
of these entities as a common enterprise,

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.420
which is no surprise given the allegations

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.260
that we've seen in the news

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.810
where basically they were
commingling all of these funds.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:14.400
So in other words, FTX, Alameda,

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.800
and many of their related
businesses were all run

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.920
by Bankman-Fried and Alameda
without any corporate controls

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.810
or separation that Bankman-Fried promised.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.030
The CFTC said that all of
this was baked into the cake.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:27.030
Quote: "FTX trading executives
also created other exceptions

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.580
to FTX's standard processes
that allowed Alameda

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.710
to have an unfair
advantage when transacting

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.290
on the platform, including
quicker execution times

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.930
and an exemption from the
platform's distinctive

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.300
auto-liquidation risk management process."

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.160
Now, all of this started
coming out in November.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.040
You can check out my earlier
video on how this came to be.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.260
In December, just before
Bankman-Fried was going

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:48.150
to testify in Congress, he was arrested

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.710
in the Bahamas, where he lives.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.540
The US Attorney's Office
for the Southern District

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.640
of New York presented
evidence to a grand jury,

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:54.960
the grand jury indicted him,

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.690
and then the US Attorney's
Office made provisional requests

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:00.300
for his arrest with the Bahamas.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.670
The US has an extradition
treaty with the Bahamas,

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:04.500
and that extradition
treaty will take a while,

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.360
but eventually he will be transferred

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.760
to the custody of the
American authorities.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.160
That means that Bankman-Fried
faces a three-headed monster

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.410
because the SEC and the CFTC's cases

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:14.760
will have broad discovery procedures,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.460
which will force Bankman-Fried
to turn over documents.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.320
He'll have to give a deposition testimony,

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.600
which could incriminate
him in the criminal case,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.190
and his lawyers will
probably ask the court

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.710
to stay the civil cases while
he defends the criminal case.

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:26.670
The judge has the discretion

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.440
to decide whether to grant that motion,

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:29.970
though don't be surprised
if Bankman-Fried ends up

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.140
taking the Fifth on a lot of this stuff,

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.210
though in the civil proceedings,

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:34.950
taking the Fifth can be used against you

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.810
and adverse inferences can be drawn

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:38.820
by your assertion of the Fifth Amendment,

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:40.680
though the SEC has more
power than the criminal court

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.420
to immediately freeze
actions by the entities

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.490
that are under the Alameda/FTX banner.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.770
And the plea agreements from
Sam Bankman-Fried's compatriots

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:48.510
paint a damning picture.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.670
Former Alameda Research
CEO Caroline Ellison

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.400
and FTX co-founder Gary
Wang have now pleaded guilty

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.410
to federal fraud charges
and are cooperating

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.000
with the Justice
Department's investigation.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.920
- I wanna make two announcements.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.750
First, I'm announcing
that the Southern District

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:03.870
of New York has filed charges
against Caroline Ellison,

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:07.326
the former CEO of Alameda
Research, and Gary Wang,

00:04:07.326 --> 00:04:10.830
a co-founder of FTX, in
connection with their roles

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:14.220
in the frauds that
contributed to FTX's collapse.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.230
Both Ms. Ellison and Mr. Wang

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:17.850
have pled guilty to those charges.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.320
- The Justice Department
made the announcement

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:20.970
on December 22nd, the same day

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:24.150
that Bankman-Fried waived
extradition to the US.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.040
Bankman-Fried is now out of jail

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:28.320
and back in his mom's
basement as a result.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.470
The federal court set bail at $250 million

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.600
and Bankman-Fried was bonded out.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.370
Now, a bail bond is usually about 10%

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.050
of the total bail amount, so
he probably had to come up

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.390
with around $25 million in assets.

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:40.980
The court record shows
that his parents will need

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.510
to post equity interest in their home

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.359
by January 12th, 2023,
and this comes just weeks

00:04:44.359 --> 00:04:47.190
after Bankman-Fried told
George Stephanopoulos

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:49.263
that he only had about $100,000
left in his bank account.

00:04:49.263 --> 00:04:51.840
Now, the bail disposition said
that two non-parent charities

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.150
would also sign bonds for lesser amounts.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:55.770
The terms of the release
require Bankman-Fried

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.900
to surrender his passport,
and he'll be confined

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.570
to detention at his parents'
home in Palo Alto, California,

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:02.880
where he will have to
wear an ankle monitor.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.740
Bankman-Fried's parents co-sign the bond

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.170
and Bankman-Fried is barred
from starting a business

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:09.540
or operating new lines of
credit more than $1,000

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.430
unless approved by the
government or the court.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:12.900
The court records show that he'll need

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.670
to undergo a mental health evaluation

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:16.860
and may request to leave
the house for exercise.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.080
Now, Caroline Ellison's
unsigned plea agreement says

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.730
that she admitted to seven counts,

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:23.490
including committing wire
fraud on FTX customers

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:25.470
and lenders of Alameda Research,

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.380
conspiracy to commit commodities
fraud and securities fraud,

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.360
and conspiracy to commit money laundering.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.010
Now, the maximum sentence of incarceration

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:33.210
on those counts is 110 years,

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:34.710
but she's not gonna
face anything like that,

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:36.060
especially because she's cooperating.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:37.710
But Ellison must continue to cooperate

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:39.630
or else she could be
charged with other crimes.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:41.700
Ellison also agreed to
the forfeiture charge,

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.290
and the government says
that it cannot agree

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:44.850
not to prosecute her for tax fraud

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.590
if those charges are appropriate.

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:47.423
By pleading guilty,

00:05:47.423 --> 00:05:49.170
Ellison is not guaranteed
a lighter sentence,

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:51.750
but typically, defendants
who cooperate completely

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.150
do get a lighter sentence,
and the plea agreements

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.250
also require her to make restitution.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:58.980
Ellison was released on bail
but cannot leave the country.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:00.990
Wang pleaded guilty to
four counts of wire fraud

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:02.370
and conspiracy to commit wire fraud,

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:04.020
commodities fraud, and securities fraud.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:05.340
The maximum sentence for those crimes

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:06.900
is about 50 years in prison.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.180
Wang also agreed to
restitution to his victims

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:10.890
and agreed to the forfeiture.

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:12.750
Now, the same cooperation
terms apply to Wang,

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.730
but he was also released
on bail with a condition

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:16.290
that he not leave the country.

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:18.810
Both plea agreements included
sections that are redacted

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.600
so as not to tip off
Bankman-Fried and other targets

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:22.980
about specific information that Ellison

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.840
and Wang shared with investigators.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:25.800
And in separate actions,

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.440
the SEC charged Wang and
Ellison with securities fraud,

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:30.480
and the CFTC also filed fraud charges

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:31.497
against Ellison and Wang.

00:06:31.497 --> 00:06:34.080
The CFTC'S amended complaint
against Bankman-Fried

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.540
adds charges against
Ellison and Wang for fraud

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:38.520
in material representations
in connection with the sale

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:41.220
of digital asset commodities
in interstate commerce,

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.620
and the CFTC hit Wang for
creating the code features

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:45.840
in the FTX trading platform
that allowed Alameda

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.970
to have an unlimited credit line on FTX

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:49.050
without being detected.

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:51.000
And the CFTC complaint offers more details

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:52.920
about what happened in
November when crypto declined

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.350
and FTX could not keep up
with customer withdrawals.

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:56.580
Publicly, Bankman-Fried claimed

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:58.410
that there was just a liquidity
problem while privately,

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:01.020
quote, "Bankman-Fried, Ellison,
and other key personnel

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.420
at FTX and Alameda acknowledged internally

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:05.280
that this shortfall
was not merely a matter

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.740
of having sufficient liquid assets on hand

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:09.630
to cover customer withdrawals
in the short term.

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:13.200
Rather, FTX customer assets
were irrevocably lost

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:15.060
because Alameda had misappropriated them."

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.490
The CFTC says that Bankman-Fried's
infamous tweet threads

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:20.250
of November 6th and 7th,
the ones where he apologized

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:21.750
for being a screw-up and accused Binance

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:24.510
of trying to sabotage FTX,
were workshopped ahead

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:27.090
of time with Ellison,
Wang, and other advisors.

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:28.477
The CFTC says, quote,

00:07:28.477 --> 00:07:30.210
"several individuals expressed concerns

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:32.550
that Bankman-Fried's tweet was inaccurate

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:33.510
and/or misleading."

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:35.280
And on November 9th,
after Binance announced

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:37.560
that it would acquire FTX in its entirety,

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:39.210
Ellison held an all-hands meeting

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:40.830
with employees of Alameda Research.

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:43.320
During the meeting, she
provided detailed information

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:44.640
about the years-long fraud.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.010
Quote: "Ellison stated
that 'starting last year,'

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:50.040
Alameda was 'borrowing a bunch
of money by open term loans'

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:53.160
and used those assets to 'make
very illiquid investments.'

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:54.720
Ellison further explained that following

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:56.850
the widespread decline
of digital asset prices,

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:58.830
most of Alameda's loans had been recalled

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.570
and, in order to meet those recalls,

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:03.000
Alameda borrowed 'a
bunch of funds' from FTX,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.540
which in turn 'led to FTX having
a shortfall in user funds.'

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:08.160
Ellison informed Alameda staff

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:09.990
that FTX had 'always allowed' Alameda

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:11.310
to borrow customer assets

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:14.370
and did not require
collateral for those loans.

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:17.580
She also explained that Alameda
could access user assets

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:19.350
without requiring FTX's approval

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:20.910
as the 'structure' allowed Alameda

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:22.500
to 'go negative in coins.'

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.060
In response to an employee question,

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:26.520
Ellison also acknowledged
that her November 6th tweet

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:29.310
to the Binance CEO offering
to buy his FTT holdings

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:33.120
at $22 per token was 'kind of
a misleading thing to tweet'

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:34.680
and expressed remorse.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:35.880
Shortly after this meeting,

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:37.770
most of Alameda's staff resigned."

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:40.110
So basically, Caroline
Ellison sort of admitted to,

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:42.900
you know, like, borrowing
a bunch of money from FTX,

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.450
being allowed to have,
like, a negative balance,

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:48.090
and that FTX and Alameda
just kept moving stuff around

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:49.410
until the bottom dropped
out of the market,

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:51.360
and then, you know, what do you know,

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:52.650
there's nothing left to borrow

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:54.900
when the customers couldn't
withdraw their money.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.310
Classic Pisces.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:57.143
One day later,

00:08:57.143 --> 00:09:00.060
FTX and 133 related entities
filed for bankruptcy.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:02.900
- I declare bankruptcy!

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:08.370
- It was all you know, very
cool, very progressive,

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:10.800
very edgy until, you know, it wasn't.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:12.840
Ellison and Wang do not
contest their liability

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.970
to the CFTC's claims, and
their testimony makes it

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:16.230
very hard for Bankman-Fried to claim

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:17.820
that he didn't know what he was doing

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:20.580
and aw, shucks, it was
just a big old mistake.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:22.020
Wang and Ellison also agreed to the entry

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:24.150
of consent orders of judgment
as to their liability

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:26.460
for engaging in massive fraud.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:27.600
The SEC complaint against Wang

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:28.830
and Ellison gives us more clues

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:31.530
about how they were cooperating
against Bankman-Fried.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:35.610
With respect to the fiat@ftx.com account,

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:38.107
the SEC said that some of
the bank accounts, quote,

00:09:38.107 --> 00:09:40.410
"were not in Alameda's
name but rather the name

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:43.230
of North Dimension Inc.,
an Alameda subsidiary.

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:45.600
North Dimension's website does
not disclose any connection

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:47.850
to Alameda," but apparently, "Ellison knew

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:49.670
that Bankman-Fried had directed FTX

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:52.050
to have customers send
funds to North Dimension

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:54.870
in an effort to hide the fact
that the funds were being sent

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:57.000
to an account controlled by Alameda."

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.580
Apparently, Alameda didn't
segregate those customer funds

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:00.810
and the North Dimension account

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:02.790
is actually the multibillion-dollar debt

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.970
referred to as the fiat@ftx.com account.

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:09.840
The fiat@ftx.com account
was then listed as a loan

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:12.270
on the quarterly balance
sheets, and Ellison allegedly

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:14.610
at Bankman-Fried's direction
combined this liability

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:15.930
with loans Alameda had received

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:17.340
from third-party lenders to obscure

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:20.130
Alameda's intertwined financial
relationship with FTX.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:22.920
Now, this information, if true,
is fairly damning evidence

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:25.260
of the defendant's intent to
mislead and conceal information

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:27.750
from investors, lenders, and customers.

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:30.930
The complaint says, "In
2022, FTX started trying

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:34.920
to separate Alameda's debt
in the fiat@ftx.com account

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:37.290
from the portion attributable to FTX."

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:39.180
And when Alameda's portion was moved

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:41.070
to a different account,
it triggered a process

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:43.230
that added interest to Alameda's debt.

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:44.460
Quote: "Bankman-Fried directed

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.740
that the Alameda liability
be moved to an account

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:48.540
that would not be charged with interest.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:50.400
This account was associated
with an individual

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:52.680
that had no apparent
connection to Alameda.

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:54.570
As a result, this change had the effect

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:56.520
of further concealing Alameda's liability

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:58.200
in FTX's internal systems."

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:00.000
And when crypto started crashing in May,

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.130
causing Alameda's lenders to come calling,

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:03.300
Bankman-Fried told Ellison

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:05.730
to hide the unlimited line of
credit on the balance sheet

00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:07.260
so that the lenders didn't know about it.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:08.820
And Ellison admitted to making statements

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:09.990
that she knew were untrue.

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:11.370
For example, in a Bloomberg article

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:13.980
from September 2022,
"Bankman-Fried claimed

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:17.070
that 'Alameda is a wholly
separate entity' than FTX,"

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:18.660
and "in the same article,
Ellison is quoted

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:21.480
as stating about Alameda,
quote, 'We're at arm's length.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:23.160
We don't get any different treatment

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:24.570
from other market makers."

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:25.770
And as for the technical issues,

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:27.690
Wang designed the so-called risk engine

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:29.850
that Bankman-Fried touted to investors.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:30.900
The SEC complaint notes

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:33.030
that Bankman-Fried
touted this risk protocol

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:34.140
when he testified to Congress.

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:36.000
Quote: "There is a complete transparency

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:37.560
about the positions that are held.

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:40.110
There's a robust consistent
risk framework applied."

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:41.160
Of course, one of the biggest problems

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:42.480
is the risk engine didn't apply

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:45.270
to FTX's biggest customer,
Alameda Research.

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:46.740
Wang knew this because he was the person

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:48.930
who created the code
exempting Alameda Research

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:51.660
from the rules and that
Ellison was aware of

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:54.090
and took advantage of this
special undisclosed benefit.

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:55.560
Of course, this is just
scratching the surface.

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:56.790
There's additional juicy details

00:11:56.790 --> 00:11:59.820
about Alameda propping
up the price of FTT,

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:02.370
FTX using bots to manipulate
the entire market,

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:05.190
and then the employees and
founders issuing giant loans

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:07.050
to each other at the company's expense.

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:08.460
Now, the SEC complaint alleges

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:09.840
that Bankman-Fried violated two parts

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:12.060
of the Securities Exchange Act of 1933,

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:14.220
breaching Section 17a and 10b.

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:15.420
Both of these sections prohibit

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:17.670
making false representations
to investors as opposed

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:20.340
to customers who would
be the exchange's users.

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:22.320
Now, the crux of the SEC's charges

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:24.690
are that Bankman-Fried
transferred billions of dollars

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:26.340
of FTX money to Alameda Research

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:27.960
while misleading FTX's investors

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:30.570
about FTX's financial
condition and risk management.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:32.610
- At the end of the day, look,

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:35.040
there's a question of
what happened and why.

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:37.110
- Alameda used this money to cover loans

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:39.240
and make additional venture
investments and personal loans

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:41.757
to Bankman-Fried and
other FTX investments.

00:12:41.757 --> 00:12:44.257
(funky music)

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:47.730
Even in November 2022, faced
with billions of dollars

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:50.460
in customer withdrawal demands
that FTX could not fulfill,

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:52.110
Bankman-Fried misled investors

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:54.090
about a multibillion-dollar hole.

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:56.460
Now, the SEC alleged that
Bankman-Fried committed fraud

00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:57.840
in the offer or sale of securities

00:12:57.840 --> 00:12:59.760
in violation of Section 17a.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:01.980
The section makes it illegal
to mislead investors.

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:04.200
Specifically, it says it's
unlawful to employ any device,

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:07.440
scheme, or artifice to defraud,
obtain money or property

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:09.570
by using material
misstatements or emissions,

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:11.370
or to engage in any transaction practice

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:13.950
or course of business which
operates or would operate

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:16.560
as a fraud or deceit upon the purchaser.

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.810
Now, the SEC typically
relies more on Rule 10b-5

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:20.790
for its enforcement
actions but in recent years

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:23.370
has stepped up prosecutions
under 17a, in part

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:25.890
because of the regulation's
weaker scienter requirements.

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:29.130
Now, scienter is another
word for the mental intent,

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:31.260
the knowledge of wrongdoing.

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:33.780
And when the SEC brings a
civil case, as it did here,

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:36.360
a violation of Section
17a can be established

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:37.890
if the defendant acted negligently,

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:40.920
in contrast to civil violation
of Rule 10b-5 requires

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:42.870
that the defendant must have
acted at least recklessly,

00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:45.660
which is a higher mental
state than just negligence.

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:47.730
The SEC says that
Bankman-Fried allowed Alameda

00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:49.470
to have a negative balance on its account

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:51.030
without any collateral requirements

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:53.730
and gave Alameda unlimited
access to credit.

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:55.380
No other account had these privileges,

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:56.490
and they were accomplished

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:58.770
by writing Alameda-specific bypasses

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:01.650
in the FTX codebase in August 2019.

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:03.510
Alameda's collateral was also allowed

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:05.100
to fall below the requisite margin levels

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:08.010
without triggering the automatic
liquidation of its account.

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:09.900
And while Bankman-Fried pulled strings

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:11.700
to give Alameda these special privileges,

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:14.490
he did not disclose any
of them to FTX investors,

00:14:14.490 --> 00:14:15.690
though news reports also suggest

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:18.000
that investors did not do
proper due diligence in FTX,

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:20.400
but perhaps that was partly
because Bankman-Fried

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:22.290
did not give the investors
much information to work with,

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:24.600
telling them to look at the
terms of service and pointing

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:27.510
to a document called FTX's
Key Principles for Ensuring

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:30.030
Investor Protections on
Digital-Asset Platforms,

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:31.957
in which FTX claimed that it, quote,

00:14:31.957 --> 00:14:33.420
"segregates customer assets

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:35.910
from its own assets across our platforms."

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:37.412
The SEC says FTX provided this document

00:14:37.412 --> 00:14:39.187
to potential investors, quote,

00:14:39.187 --> 00:14:41.918
"including a US investor
who invested $35 million

00:14:41.918 --> 00:14:45.600
in FTX's Series B fundraising
round in July 2021."

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:46.740
But these statements were false,

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:49.890
as FTX did not segregate customer
assets from its own assets

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:51.480
and, as events would later demonstrate,

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:52.710
did not maintain liquidity

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:54.630
to allow customer withdrawals on demand.

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:57.240
Now, FTX did provide audited financials,

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:58.980
but those statements
don't include information

00:14:58.980 --> 00:15:01.020
about Alameda's
undocumented line of credit

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:02.760
from FTX or the other information

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:04.800
about its special deals with Alameda.

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:06.030
Bankman-Fried told investors

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:08.280
that Alameda did not get
any special treatment

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:10.200
and that he told his
executives to emphasize

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:12.480
that FTX and Alameda were
two separate entities.

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:14.730
The SEC says that all of that
was materially misleading,

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:17.130
and that seems kind of
like a slam-dunk case.

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:18.240
Now, the SEC also alleges

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:20.280
that Bankman-Fried violated Section 10b-5,

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:21.870
the more traditional fraud provisions

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:23.580
that are used in a situation like this.

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:26.250
The SEC alleges that Bankman-Fried
used creative accounting

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:28.890
and little documentation
to hide his personal loans.

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:29.827
According to the complaint, quote,

00:15:29.827 --> 00:15:32.760
"between March 2020 and September 2022,

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:34.860
Bankman-Fried executed
promissory notes for loans

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:39.180
from Alameda totaling
more than $1.338 billion,

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:40.110
including two instances

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:42.090
in which Bankman-Fried
was both the borrower

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:44.160
in his individual capacity and the lender

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:46.800
in his capacity as CEO of Alameda."

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:48.480
Bankman-Fried used these loans

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:50.970
to make political contributions
and buy real estate

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:53.610
for himself, his parents,
and other FTX executives,

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:55.447
and besides all of this,
the SEC alleges that, quote,

00:15:55.447 --> 00:15:57.690
"the record keeping regarding
the purchase and ownership

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:00.330
of real estate was poorly
organized and documented.

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:02.430
Neither the fact of the
loans and the purchases

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:03.870
nor the poor documentation

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:05.190
of significant company liabilities

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:08.400
and expenditures was
disclosed to investors."

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:10.560
- So you do know and you did know

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:14.790
that FDX deposits were
being funneled to Alameda.

00:16:14.790 --> 00:16:18.900
- So I was vaguely aware that that was how

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:22.050
some wires were being
sent in the first place.

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:23.580
- Now, I'm sensing a theme here.

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:26.370
Now, the SEC also cites
a September 2022 meeting

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:28.110
for US investors in which FTX claimed,

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:30.030
quote, "outside of BlockFi,

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:32.400
we didn't increase our
exposure to crypto."

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:34.500
That statement was, quote,
"false and misleading."

00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:36.247
Bankman-Fried knew that as a result,

00:16:36.247 --> 00:16:39.780
"FTX's exposure to crypto,
including its own FTT token,

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:42.240
increased substantially as
Alameda increased its borrowing,

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:44.070
backed by FTT as collateral."

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:46.530
Now, the CFTC lawsuit is a
civil enforcement action,

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:48.720
which means that it isn't
charging Bankman-Fried criminally.

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:50.550
That's what the DOJ is doing.

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:52.530
But the complaint seeks disgorgement,

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:54.900
i.e., paying back of all
the ill-gotten gains,

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:57.570
civil penalties, a ban
on Bankman-Fried acting

00:16:57.570 --> 00:16:59.460
as an officer or director of a company,

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:01.860
and to prohibit him from
participating in the issuance,

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:04.980
purchase, offer, or sale of
any securities in the future.

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:06.810
Now, that takes us to the CFTC complaint.

00:17:06.810 --> 00:17:08.670
Now, I told you before
that the CFTC believed

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:11.130
that it had the authority to
regulate crypto as a commodity,

00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:12.870
and its complaint against Bankman-Fried

00:17:12.870 --> 00:17:14.160
and FTX makes no bones about it.

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:16.200
Quote: "Certain digital
assets are commodities,

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:18.810
including bitcoin, ether,
tether, and others,

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:21.990
as defined under the
Commodity Exchange Act."

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:24.570
Thus, the agency filed a
complaint naming Bankman-Fried,

00:17:24.570 --> 00:17:28.290
FTX Trading Ltd., and Elevated
Research LLC as defendants.

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:30.720
Much of the CFTC'S
complaint reiterates facts

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:32.820
let out concurrently
in the SEC's complaint,

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:34.770
including all the stuff
about the special treatment

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:37.110
of Alameda Research that it
got from FTX and the fact

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:40.530
that Alameda Research served
as a limitless slush fund

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:42.930
for Bankman-Fried and
other FTX executives.

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:45.090
In fact, the CFTC says that
these built-in advantages

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:47.460
for Alameda, quote, "directly
contradicted public statements

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:49.620
made by and on behalf of defendants."

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:51.870
This allowed Alameda to use FTX assets

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:53.760
whenever it needed to
pay off its own lenders.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:56.610
The CFTC says that FTX also
made a giant no-interest loan

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:58.110
to Alameda, which it concealed

00:17:58.110 --> 00:17:59.640
with the now-infamous line item

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:02.037
on the balance sheet called fiat@.

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:04.710
Quote: "During the relevant period,

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:06.660
this account held a balance of as much

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:08.850
as $8 billion in customer funds."

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:09.840
Now, that wasn't the only time

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:11.730
that Bankman-Fried used a weird trick

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:13.470
your accountant doesn't
want you to know about

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:15.630
to hide an enormous liability.

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:18.180
Quote: "At least in part
to remediate the risk

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:20.490
that Alameda's large
liability would be discovered,

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:22.500
at Bankman-Fried's
discretion, FTX executives

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:25.140
reallocated Alameda's
approximately $1 billion

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:28.770
in liabilities to a customer
account on FTX's system

00:18:28.770 --> 00:18:30.240
that Bankman-Fried would later refer to

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:32.850
as 'our Korean friend's account'

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:35.610
and/or 'the weird Korean account.'

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:37.560
This was technically a
sub-account of Alameda,

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:39.990
but unlike other Alameda
sub-accounts on FTX,

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:44.040
it was not opened under a
@alamedaresearch.com identifier

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:46.140
and was not otherwise readily identifiable

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:48.870
as being an Alameda-associated account."

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:51.930
This, in plain terms, seems like fraud.

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:54.900
The CFTC charged Bankman-Fried
with violating Section 6c

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:56.190
of the Commodities Exchange Act,

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:58.710
which is basically a
pattern after Rule 10b

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:00.120
of the Securities and Exchange Act.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:02.310
The law is a broad sort
of catch-all provision

00:19:02.310 --> 00:19:04.260
that prohibits fraud in all of its forms,

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:06.930
and the law prohibits
manipulative and deceptive devices

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:11.070
and contrivances in connection
with any swap or contract,

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:13.770
basically anything in relation
to the sale of a commodity.

00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:16.020
Now, the newest amendments
to the law were added in 2010

00:19:16.020 --> 00:19:18.150
by the Dodd-Frank Act,
which make it easier

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:19.560
for the CFTC to charge fraud

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:21.150
because the government
no longer has to prove

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:22.680
that a defendant specifically intended

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:25.260
to create a price trend
that was manipulative.

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:28.530
Now, the CFTC can charge
anyone who uses manipulative

00:19:28.530 --> 00:19:31.770
or deceptive devices as
intentionally reckless in conduct

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:34.890
that's intended to create or
did create an artificial price.

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:36.630
Now, that takes us to
the criminal indictment

00:19:36.630 --> 00:19:38.550
from the Department of Justice.

00:19:38.550 --> 00:19:40.560
The US Attorney's Office
for the Southern District

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:43.080
of New York, which handles
a lot of financial crimes

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:45.540
because New York City is
within the Southern District

00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:48.540
of New York, and they
obtained an indictment

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:51.240
against Bankman-Fried for
conspiracy to commit wire fraud,

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:53.400
wire fraud conspiracy to
commit commodities fraud,

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:55.320
conspiracy to commit securities fraud,

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:56.970
conspiracy to commit money laundering,

00:19:56.970 --> 00:19:59.310
and conspiracy to defraud the
Federal Election Commission

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:01.590
and commit campaign finance violations.

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:03.810
Now, we kicked off our first
video about Bankman-Fried

00:20:03.810 --> 00:20:05.400
by discussing the
possibility of wire fraud,

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:06.570
and what do you know?

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:07.950
It was wire fraud.

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:09.240
Wire fraud specifically involves the use

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:10.860
of telecommunications or
online communications,

00:20:10.860 --> 00:20:12.660
including emails, social
media, text messages,

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:14.580
or even good old-fashioned fax machines.

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:16.890
The unsealed indictment
says that starting in 2019

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:19.103
and continuing until November of 2022,

00:20:19.103 --> 00:20:21.300
Bankman-Fried committed wire fraud.

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:23.370
Now, this might not have
come as a complete shock

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:24.420
to Bankman-Fried, because according

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:26.310
to the Australian
website Financial Review,

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:28.860
Bankman-Fried and his FTX
and Alameda associates

00:20:28.860 --> 00:20:32.940
had a secret chat group
called, cryptically, Wirefraud.

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:35.040
The group exchanged
secret messages on Signal

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:38.130
about the potential for US wire
fraud charges for their role

00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:40.590
in the implosion of FTX
and Alameda Research.

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:41.850
In response to the allegation,

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:43.890
Bankman-Fried tweeted, "If this is true,

00:20:43.890 --> 00:20:46.590
then I wasn't a member
of that inner circle.

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:47.850
I'm quite sure it's just false.

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:49.290
I have never heard of such a group."

00:20:49.290 --> 00:20:50.790
Though, on the other
hand, maybe he just wanted

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:52.620
to save the Department of
Justice a whole bunch of money

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:54.930
in making it super easy to prosecute him

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:56.280
and everyone that he worked with.

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:57.113
And as I mentioned,

00:20:57.113 --> 00:20:58.560
the government also criminally
charged Bankman-Fried

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:00.780
with various species of
conspiracy to commit wire fraud,

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:02.880
securities fraud, commodities fraud,

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:04.830
money laundering, and
campaign finance violations,

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:06.690
and regarding that last
charge, the prosecutor said

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:08.370
that Bankman-Fried misappropriated tens

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:10.200
of millions of dollars of customer funds

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:12.540
to make illegal donations
to both Democratic

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:13.980
and Republican candidates.

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:16.110
So the question is, if
Bankman-Fried is convicted,

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:17.490
how long is he going to jail?

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:19.980
Well, the CFTC and the
SEC filed civil complaints

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:21.780
seeking monetary damages
and injunctive relief

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:24.210
to prevent Bankman-Fried
from being near a corporation

00:21:24.210 --> 00:21:25.680
or a company ever again.

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:27.360
Now, the fines could be
enormous in this case,

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:29.070
but it'll probably take
a long time to resolve

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:30.720
with potentially years of discovery

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:33.120
and a string of bankruptcies
to sort things through.

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:34.380
The criminal case filed by the Department

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:35.910
of Justice will go much faster.

00:21:35.910 --> 00:21:37.560
Now, Bankman-Fried was
indicted for a number

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:39.180
of different federal felonies,

00:21:39.180 --> 00:21:40.620
some of which meld into each other,

00:21:40.620 --> 00:21:42.720
but some are separate counts.

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:44.700
And unlike the CFTC
and the SEC complaints,

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:47.640
if the DOJ convicts, Sam
Bankman-Fried could be looking

00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:48.780
at a long sentence.

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:51.060
The amount of time he gets
depends on two things:

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:52.680
the offenses of conviction

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:54.660
and the federal sentencing guidelines.

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:57.180
The sentencing guidelines
provide 43 levels

00:21:57.180 --> 00:21:58.140
of offense seriousness.

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:01.260
The more serious the crime,
the higher the offense level,

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:03.840
and each type of crime is
assigned a base offense level,

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:06.600
which is the starting point
for determining the seriousness

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:07.650
of the particular offense.

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:09.510
Now, Bankman-Fried is
not a prior offender,

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:11.280
which would normally reduce the sentence,

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:13.410
but the nature of his crimes is serious,

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:14.670
and there are at least
three different groups

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:17.040
of victims here: lenders, customers,

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:18.420
and the United States government.

00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:20.700
Now, wire fraud carries
a maximum prison sentence

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:23.370
of up to 20 years, and if
the wire fraud involves

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:27.330
over $550 million, which is
possible if not likely here,

00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:29.460
then Bankman-Fried could
get a maximum sentence,

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:31.530
even without a prior criminal history.

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:33.390
Securities fraud carries
a maximum sentence

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:35.070
of up to 25 years in prison and a fine

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:36.540
that is based on the amount of money

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:39.930
that was fraudulently
obtained, which here was a lot,

00:22:39.930 --> 00:22:41.940
and a conviction for money
laundering could result

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:44.400
in a sentence of up to
20 years and a big fine.

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:45.840
So if we look at it conservatively,

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:47.220
assuming the government proves some

00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:48.240
but not all of the charges,

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:50.610
Bankman-Fried could get a
sentence of 10 to 15 years.

00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:51.990
There's still a lot of
things that we don't know

00:22:51.990 --> 00:22:54.210
since the case is still
in its very early stages,

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:56.070
and it could be a lot more than that.

